es in a machine learning  talk the answer is a resounding yes  nowadays virtual machines have gotten  way bigger compute engine allows you to  configure up machines with up to four  terabytes of memory  not disk memory and it's even got the  compute power to pair with it so we can  do something with that memory and on the  software side libraries like desk allow  you to paralyze your work in pandas  without needing to have a cluster of  multiple machines set up even at big  companies the large data sets being used  they end up typically being less than 14  gigabytes and even for truly large data  sets you can often just take a subset of  that data for experimentation so don't  underestimate the cost of losing out on  what David calls data agility the full  talk offers up a great resource for  helping you take advantage of this kind  of burst training with a script that  sets up a machine run your job and then  cleans it all up afterwards definitely  check that out if you're looking to try  out this mode of operating the final  portion of the talk covers cloud ml  engine if your service relies on  predictions or if you regularly need to  retrain your model on new data cloud ml  engine can help in scikit-learn and  actually boost are also now supported in  addition to tensorflow and it's for both  training and prediction so you don't  need to use different workflows for  different machine learning libraries the  full talk has a pretty slick  top-to-bottom demo of pre-processing the  data training a scikit-learn model and  then exporting that model and finally  deploying it to cloud ml engine all in  one notebook if you like this video give  it a thumbs up and I've included the  link to the full talk in the description  below and don't forget to subscribe to  the Google cloud YouTube channel for  more great content I'll see you on the  next rewind  [Music]
 

